[patterns/application/micro-frontend/PTN-APP-MFE-009-shell-provided-props-pattern.md#variation-1-server-side-rendering-ssr-with-props-injection]

#### Variation 1: Server-Side Rendering (SSR) with Props Injection

**Use Case**: Next.js or Remix applications where modules need props during server-side rendering.

**Implementation:**

```typescript
// shell/src/pages/_app.tsx (Next.js)
import { AppProps } from 'next/app';
import { MfeContext } from '@gainwell/mfe-types';

export async function getServerSideProps(context) {
  // Fetch context server-side
  const mfeContext = await fetchMfeContextServerSide(context.req);

  return {
    props: { mfeContext },
  };
}

export default function App({ Component, pageProps, mfeContext }: AppProps & { mfeContext: MfeContext }) {
  return (
    <MfeContextProvider value={mfeContext}>
      <Component {...pageProps} />
    </MfeContextProvider>
  );
}

// Module consumes via React Context
export function ClaimsPage() {
  const context = useMfeContext();
  // Use context.user, context.config, etc.
}
```

**Trade-offs:**
- ✅ Faster initial render (no client-side bootstrap delay)
- ✅ SEO-friendly (props available during SSR)
- ❌ Requires server-side session management
- ❌ More complex deployment (server + CDN)

