[patterns/application/micro-frontend/PTN-APP-MFE-009-shell-provided-props-pattern.md#variation-4-hybrid-props--event-bus]

#### Variation 4: Hybrid Props + Event Bus

**Use Case**: Combine static props (user, config) with dynamic events (notifications, updates) for flexibility.

**Implementation:**

```typescript
// shell/src/bootstrap.tsx
import { eventBus } from '@gainwell/event-bus';

export async function bootstrapApplication(): Promise<MfeContext> {
  const context = await fetchStaticContext();

  // Listen for dynamic updates
  eventBus.subscribe('user:profileUpdated', (newProfile) => {
    console.log('User profile updated:', newProfile);
    // Trigger app reload or update state
  });

  return {
    ...context,
    eventBus,  // Provide event bus as part of context
  };
}

// Modules consume static props + subscribe to dynamic events
export function ClaimsApp({ context }: { context: MfeContext }) {
  const { user, config, eventBus } = context;

  useEffect(() => {
    const unsubscribe = eventBus.subscribe('featureFlags:updated', (newFlags) => {
      // Handle feature flag updates without full reload
      setLocalFeatureFlags(newFlags);
    });

    return unsubscribe;
  }, [eventBus]);

  // Use user, config as read-only props
}
```

**Trade-offs:**
- ✅ Best of both worlds: static props for immutable data, event bus for dynamic updates
- ✅ No app reload required for feature flag updates
- ❌ Increased complexity (two communication mechanisms)
- ❌ Requires Event Bus pattern implementation (PTN-APP-MFE-008)

**When to Use Each Variation:**
- **SSR Props Injection**: Use for Next.js/Remix apps prioritizing SEO and initial render speed
- **Lazy Context**: Use when bootstrap time is critical and not all modules need all props
- **Multi-Level Contexts**: Use when different module groups need different security contexts
- **Hybrid Props + Event Bus**: Use when you need static bootstrap data AND dynamic runtime updates

