[patterns/application/micro-frontend/PTN-APP-MFE-009-shell-provided-props-pattern.md#anti-patterns]

### Anti-Patterns

**❌ Anti-Pattern 1: Mutating Props**

```typescript
// ❌ NEVER DO THIS
export function ClaimsApp({ context }: { context: MfeContext }) {
  // Mutating context props
  context.user.displayName = "Updated Name";  // WRONG!
  context.featureFlags.newFlag = true;         // WRONG!

  // This breaks immutability and affects other modules
}
```

**✅ Correct Approach**: Treat props as read-only; copy if mutation needed for local state.

**❌ Anti-Pattern 2: Storing Props in localStorage**

```typescript
// ❌ NEVER DO THIS
export const mount: MountFunction = async (container, context) => {
  // Storing context in localStorage
  localStorage.setItem('mfeContext', JSON.stringify(context));  // WRONG!

  // Security risk: exposes tokens/permissions; stale data on next load
};
```

**✅ Correct Approach**: Use props directly; shell provides fresh data on each load.

**❌ Anti-Pattern 3: Fetching User Data Despite Shell Providing It**

```typescript
// ❌ NEVER DO THIS
export function ClaimsApp({ context }: { context: MfeContext }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Fetching user again despite context.user available
    fetchUser().then(setUser);  // WRONG! Redundant API call
  }, []);

  // Use context.user instead!
}
```

**✅ Correct Approach**: Use `context.user` directly; trust shell-provided data.

