[patterns/application/micro-frontend/PTN-APP-MFE-009-shell-provided-props-pattern.md#phi-protection-in-shell-provided-props]

#### PHI Protection in Shell-Provided Props

**Low Risk Pattern**: Shell-Provided Props is the **safest integration pattern** for HIPAA compliance because:
1. Props are read-only (cannot be modified by modules)
2. Shell controls what data is provided (centralized review)
3. Props are immutable (no risk of accidental PHI injection)

**Safe Data Categories for Props:**

✅ **Allowed in Props:**

- User identity (userId, email, displayName)
- User roles and permissions (claims_processor, provider_admin)
- Application configuration (API URLs, feature flags, theme)
- Session metadata (sessionId, loginTimestamp)
- Non-PHI context (stateCode for state-specific configurations)

❌ **PROHIBITED in Props:**

- Patient/member PHI (member names, SSNs, dates of birth)
- Clinical data (diagnoses, procedures, medications)
- Claims details (claim amounts, provider information)
- Provider credentials (NPI, license numbers)
- Any data subject to HIPAA Privacy Rule

**Safe MfeContext Example:**

```typescript
// ✅ SAFE: Shell-Provided Props with NO PHI
export interface MfeContext {
  user: {
    userId: string;           // ✅ User identity (not member PHI)
    email: string;            // ✅ User contact
    displayName: string;      // ✅ User name (staff, not patient)
    roles: string[];          // ✅ Authorization
    permissions: string[];    // ✅ Access control
    stateCode?: string;       // ✅ Non-PHI context
  };
  config: {
    apiBaseUrl: string;       // ✅ Configuration
    environment: string;      // ✅ Configuration
    featureFlags: Record<string, boolean>; // ✅ Configuration
  };
  theme: {
    mode: 'light' | 'dark';   // ✅ UI preference
    primaryColor: string;     // ✅ UI preference
  };
}
```

**Unsafe MfeContext Example (NEVER DO THIS):**

```typescript
// ❌ FORBIDDEN: PHI in Props
export interface MfeContext {
  user: {
    userId: string;
    currentMember?: {         // ❌ FORBIDDEN!
      memberId: string;
      firstName: string;      // ❌ PHI!
      lastName: string;       // ❌ PHI!
      dateOfBirth: string;    // ❌ PHI!
      ssn: string;            // ❌ PHI!
    };
  };
}
```

**Build-Time PHI Detection:**

```typescript
// packages/mfe-types/scripts/validate-phi.ts
import * as ts from 'typescript';

const PHI_FIELD_PATTERNS = [
  /firstName|lastName|fullName|patientName/i,
  /dateOfBirth|dob|birthDate/i,
  /ssn|socialSecurity/i,
  /diagnosis|procedure|medication|condition/i,
  /medicalRecordNumber|mrn/i,
  /claim(?!Id)/i, // "claim" but not "claimId"
];

function validateMfeContextInterface(sourceFile: ts.SourceFile) {
  function visit(node: ts.Node) {
    if (ts.isInterfaceDeclaration(node) && node.name.text === 'MfeContext') {
      node.members.forEach(member => {
        if (ts.isPropertySignature(member) && member.name) {
          const propName = member.name.getText();

          for (const pattern of PHI_FIELD_PATTERNS) {
            if (pattern.test(propName)) {
              throw new Error(
                `PHI field detected in MfeContext: "${propName}". ` +
                `Props MUST NOT contain PHI per HIPAA Security Rule.`
              );
            }
          }
        }
      });
    }

    ts.forEachChild(node, visit);
  }

  visit(sourceFile);
}

// Run in CI/CD: npm run validate-phi
```

**Security Requirements:**
- **Type Safety**: All props MUST use TypeScript interfaces with strict typing enabled (`strict: true` in tsconfig.json).
- **Runtime Validation**: Modules MUST validate props at mount time using a validation library (Zod, Yup, or similar) to catch contract violations.
- **Immutability Enforcement**: Modules MUST treat props as `readonly`. Use `Readonly<MfeContext>` type or `Object.freeze()` to enforce immutability.

